 watch the  action and get caught up in the fun  being had by the Carefree adolescents  in fact as we would expect from the  passions roused by football matches  today medieval authorities often move to  ban these games at times of urban unrest  or particularly bitter rivalries between  teams in the 14th century for example  the Oxford coroner had to investigate  the circumstances in which a student had  been killed in the course of a football  game between his friends and a group of  Irish students this could mean either  that this form of the game was more  dangerous like the rough sport later  associated with the grammar school  founded at rugby in the 16th century or  that something had occurred during the  game to become a catalyst for violence  in 1314 to take another example the  mayor of London issued a decree  prohibiting a variety of sports and  pastimes that were known to disturb the  peace on the grounds that King Edward II  was embarking on a war against the Scots  and had charged his officials to  maintain order in his absence  as a result the carrying of arms was  forbidden within City walls even for use  in fencing schools and there was a  specific ban on football games the  decree originally in anglo-french reads  and because there is great uproar in the  city due to certain rajari de gross  pelot to pay that is big heated football  matches being played on common land from  which many evils can arise from time to  time he forbade that these games be  played within the city precincts  however the wording of this injunction  would have been a clear indication to  his listeners that it was okay to play  outside the city across the Thames where  other forbidden looty were traditionally  allowed  ball games as a test of skill and  accuracy could also take the form of  bowling for which there are terms in a  range of medieval languages  and a related category of sports  involved competitive projectile throwing  from horseshoes to hammers to coits  these games too could go awry another  Coroner's report this one from London in  1275 reported the two friends called  John futard and John Leclerc quote were  playing together with their tiles in the  churchyard when the latter throwing the  tile in his turn and quite against his  own will struck the said John futard on  the right side of the head making a  wound two inches long and penetrating to  the brain  this unfortunate John died six days  later there is no record of the verdict  against his friend but it was probably  ruled involuntary manslaughter  board games and games of chance were  also of enormous importance during this  period crowned by that other medieval  sport of Kings chess  in my earlier lecture on accounting and  risk we were introduced to the  checkerboard cloth of the English  exchequer where counters representing  sums of money were tallied and stacked  games of checkers or drafts also using  counters have been played for much of  human history but it was only in the  Middle Ages that this board became the  stage of Epic and sometimes never-ending  or unresolved battles between two Royal  miniature armies  the earliest written accounts of Chess  in medieval Sanskrit and middle Persian  give it similar names chaturanga in  Sanskrit and satrang in Persian both  referring to the four limbs or divisions  of an army which in those lands included  elephants and chariots or camels as well  as Cavalry and Infantry  the names for chess and Arabic and Greek  are obviously related and zatrician  the Latin term ludus kakoram or scotchi  and its vernacular derivatives had been  tied to a different Persian word Shah  King yielding both check and chess  the etymology of mate is more contested  with some historical linguists pointing  to the Persian mate which means helpless  or abandoned While others related to the  Arabic is dead  that chess had already been well  established in the medieval  Mediterranean World by the early 11th  century is clear from a letter of  complaint written by Peter Damien a  theologian and Bishop of ostia the port  of Rome in which he officiously informs  another high-ranking Churchman that he  had happened upon the bishop of Florence  absorbed in a game of chess Damien had  rebuked his fellow bishop and insisted  that church canons forbidding games of  chance like dice also applied to chess  but gerardus the Florentine Bishop so  accused retorted the chess was a game of  skill  another early indication of the game's  adoption in Italy is the so-called chess  set of Charlemagne made from elephant  Ivory which actually dates from the 11th  century and is thus contemporary with  Peter Damien's anecdote the famous Lewis  Chessman found on that hebridean island  in Scotland are made of walrus Ivory and  were probably crafted in Norway during  the 12th century evidence of how quickly  the game had traveled from the  Mediterranean to the northernmost  reaches of Europe  as just took hold in medieval Europe the  distinctive appearance of its pieces  their movements and the rules of the  game began taking on the forms familiar  today via a relatively rapid process of  codification that indicates how  widespread and Universal the game had  become  European influence is most significant  in the substitution of the queen for the  figure of the vizier the advisor or  fixer of a king who could only move one  square diagonally at a time  this replacement had begun to occur by  the beginning of the 11th century with  the word vizier becoming an old French  fear Proud Lady within the next few  centuries moreover the queen had become  the most powerful figure on the board  moving fluidly and mirroring the tricky  movements of a powerful woman's  political maneuvers  in addition to the king the most stable  pieces over time were those of the  horsemen or Knight and pawn whose  deliberate but vulnerable pacing matched  the slow movements of medieval  infantrymen meanwhile in Europe the  Asian elephant became a bishop in  English a fool in French or a Herald  Alfieri in Italian but its role remained  the same The indo-persian Chariot or  Arab camel Rook became in Europe LaRoche  or Rook reflecting the new importance of  fortified castles in the 11th century  indeed it's clear that part of chess's  Allure derived from the ways that it  mimicked or commented on the roles of  various categories of people in medieval  society which made chess a metaphor not  only for Warfare and kingship but for  political strategy business dealings and  seduction  chess games are featured in thousands of  extant medieval artworks from Ivory  carvings to manuscript illuminations to  love poems chess was also an apt  allegory for life lessons in the 13th  century a Dominican Friar called yakopo  of Chase Olay an aptly named town in the  Piedmont region of Italy composed a  Latin Treatise entitled on human morals  and Noble duties according to the game  of chess  a text which was soon translated into  French as Le Judes  the moralized game of chess  yoga po offers a fanciful origin story  for the game claiming that it was  invented by a Greek philosopher in  probably named Xerxes  according to the Middle English  translation of William caxton published  in 1474 the philosopher wanted to quote  teach the manners and conditions of  kings of Nobles and of the common people  and of their offices and how they should  be touched and drawn that is handled and  influenced end quote the philosopher  also wanted to teach the reader how he  should amend himself and become virtuous  after explaining the setup and rules of  the game yakopo then devotes a section  of the text to the role of each piece  and how it teaches us about its human  counterpart in society he even gives  each Pawn a separate identity  corresponding to the types of commoners  in a medieval city-state laborers Smiths  notaries Merchants Physicians innkeepers  Watchmen and entertainers  then goes on to discuss the virtues and  vices common to each class of people and  the ways that a good ruler can make laws  and use his influence for the betterment  of society  a gorgeously illuminated copy of this  work now in the Vatican Library shows  yakopo preaching from a Pulpit adorned  with a large chessboard and his audience  wrapped with interest  many representations of medieval chess  matches suggest that the game was also a  spectator sport and an opportunity for  players to perform and show off the  newest Fashions the success of the  Netflix series the Queen's Gambit  suggests that not much has changed in  this regard either  in one illumination a lavishly dressed  party of young people watched two of  their number play a game for apparently  high stakes and older men in the  background hoists a sack full of money  on which the female player casts an  anxious glance her pet squirrel at her  feet  her male opponent reaches for a piece on  the board as his little dog Snuffles  around him and a tense couple she with  her arms folded he hugging her from  behind are mirrored by a couple on the  left both equally riveted on the game  she whispering in his ear on the far  right of this image another man has his  sword out and is making eye contact with  the older man wielding the sack another  indication that a lot is riding on the  outcome of this game  the association of chess with gambling  which was hugely popular at all echelons  of medieval Society helps to explain the  discomforture with which Elite clergy  regarded this past time especially if it  were played for money or Wagers  as noted above Peter Damien had accused  a fellow Bishop of engaging in a sinful  act when he played a game of chess he  had even warned that canon law allowed  for the deposition of dicing Bishops  Alia Torres episcopy but the accused  cleric was indignant in his own defense  saying one is chess the other is Dice so  if that church Authority prohibited dice  it must have allowed chess precisely  because it remains silent about it  to which retort Damian had fired back  chess I said to him isn't cited in the  scriptures either but the word dice  includes both types of games  and Peter Damien may have been correct  in this some Scholars have suggested  that the earliest form of Chess as  played in India had involved the flowing  of dice it may be that this practice  continued to be a variant of the game as  it made its way Westward the first  detailed description of the game  certainly shows that it could be adapted  in many different ways this account  comes from an extraordinary illuminated  manuscript the Libro de las juegos or  book of games compiled for Alfonso the  tenth El Sabio the wise king of Castile  and completed at the Royal scriptorium  in 1283.  because of the close ties between this  Kingdom and the Muslim polities of  alandalus and Alfonso's well-documented  campaign to Translate Arabic texts into  the Castilian vernacular this compendium  is very likely to preserve oral and  written gaming Traditions passed down  for centuries  for example it includes the earliest  known description of backgammon as well  as in illustration and description of  nine men's Morris a game similar to  Tic-tac-toe and dating back at least to  ancient Rome where it was popular among  soldiers and common people because the  board could be quickly sketched in the  dirt and any small objects could be used  as playing pieces  this Deluxe codex begins with a story  honoring the genealogy of Chess which is  said to have been invented by an Indian  philosopher king a more plausible  version of the tale related by yakopo  who had also stressed the game's moral  and political uses  this manuscript features more than 100  Illustrated chess problems and solutions  as well as variants for multiple players  including a game of astronomical chess  played on a special board representing  the zodiac in its entirety then the book  reflects Alfonso's fascination with  gaming as a dialectical exercise that  pits a player's intelligence and skill  against the forces of Fortune and fate  as well as the Wiles of his opponent  for the many medieval critics of gaming  however there was no such sophisticated  dialectic to justify play dice cards and  other games were condemned as  Blasphemous pastimes that subverted the  order of God's creation and made a  mockery of church doctrine this was a  favorite Topic in the preaching of a  fiery 15th century Franciscan reformer  Bernardino of Siena in one Vivid sermon  fra Bernardino imagines a set of  gamblers as participants in a diabolic  Black Mass the priest is the Devil  Himself and his missile is a set of dice  whose markings are made with his own  excrement the playing cards are the  devil's Breviary just look at them clubs  weapons wielded by crazy Savages cops  for drunkards coins for the greedy and  swords for killers  the two-headed Kings and the Queens are  assorted Lords of misrule turning the  world upside down  rather than offering prayers Gamers sigh  for their losses instead of taking  communion in the Eucharist they become  drunk cries of Glory to God are drowned  out by the groans of losers and the  shouts of I win and when the Unholy  masses ended Bernardino warns you will  see the evil you have done and may  rather than living in Christ kill  yourself in despair  it was thus the Unholy passions aroused  by gambling as well as the iniquities of  gambling and greed that arouse the  preacher's ire and concern a manuscript  illumination from this same period  captures the mood of a dice game that  has clearly inspired intense emotions  among its onlookers and players at least  one of whom seems about to lose his  shirt  dice are not medieval inventions they  are as old in one form or another as  civilization but cards for gaming and  divination do appear to be a medieval  Innovation beginning in China around the  9th century and making their way to  Western Asia by the 11th examples from  medieval Persia suggest that there were  a range of different suits or patterns  with 12 cards originally in each 10 pip  cards and two court cards usually a king  and vizier as in contemporary chess sets  from the region  the oldest surviving deck dating from  the early 15th century and made in Egypt  shows that each suit had by then come to  include 13 cards just like modern decks  with a third figure the vizier's deputy  among the court cards the four most  common suits cups swords coins and  batons are still found in some European  traditions and are also familiar from  Modern tarot cards this word from the  Italian tarochi whose etymology is  obscure was originally another type of  gaming deck supplemented by additional  cards painted with elaborate allegorical  or astrological scenes first documented  in Northern Italy these decks became  known as triomphi or in English trumps  until the invention of printing which  enabled the mass production of cards to  the horror of clerical critics Dex would  have been specially commissioned and  produced for individual patrons a  surviving account from the Allied  duchies of brabant and Luxembourg  records with apparent disapproval that  the Duke and Duchess had paid Assam  amounting to the price of eight and a  half sheep for a mere deck of cards the  oldest extant European sets are the  deluxe remnants of some 15 incomplete  Decks that were made in the mid-15th  century for Filippo Maria Visconti Duke  of Milan whom a contemporary Source  credits with having invented novum  quadum and exquisite news a rather new  and exquisite kind of Trumps  cards from these decks are now dispersed  among many public and private  Collections and they feature designs  resembling the Arcana of modern tarot  decks death the emperor and Empress the  fool the Knight of Cups and so on  the earliest printed decks not  surprisingly were manufactured in some  of the first German towns to set up  printing presses and reveal that many of  the Arabic Persian Latin suits had been  replaced in northern Europe by Hearts  Shields acorns leaves and bells  the oldest complete set of cards from  this period is the Flemish hunting deck  hand-drawn rather than printed with the  suits all featuring hunting themes horns  leashes and collars for hounds and  nooses for game  meanwhile as in chess the Christian  Court cards vizier had become the queen  and his Deputy the knave or Jack  there isn't space here to detail or even  name all of the local gaming variants  and outdoor seasonal sports that  developed throughout the Medieval World  and that are still the traditional focus  of regional festivals and Heritage  reenactments like the Highland Games of  Scotland the very fact that communities  cherish their own forms of play or house  rules that gov that govern local  varieties of sport is a testament to the  enormous importance of play in the life  of every society  and while not all of the games people  played during the Middle Ages were  Medieval in origin it is thanks to  Medieval sources and surviving artifacts  that we know so much about the history  of pre-modern Ludi not incidentally it  is thanks to a medievalist the Dutch  historian johannsinka that we have  recognized scholarly disciplines such as  game studies Game Theory and the history  of sport all derived from his massively  influential book homo ludens first  published in Dutch in 1938  as its title suggests hoisinger regarded  the species homo human to be more  accurately characterized as ludens  playing rather than sapiens  understanding or reasoning this is as he  argued because play is older than  culture and therefore more Elemental to  The Human Condition than other  attributes that have been induced as  essential to human survival such as  speech play hoytzinga insists  constitutes its own form of life not  real life or ordinary life but a state  of freedom and creativity that is  ultimately the generator of all art and  of all that is good in human society  as he put it civilization quote does not  come from play like a baby detaching  itself from the womb it arises in play  and as play and never leaves it  what better way to describe the  longevity and endurance of the games we  still play
 

